{"records":[{"id":"4hwv29mhcb","title":"Gamma-butyrobetaine hydroxylase 1 confers metastatic advantage by shielding cancer cells from natural killer cell immunosurveillance","description":"Primary tumors constantly shed cancer cells into the circulation, yet only a fraction of these cells manage to give rise to metastatic tumors. Success appears to depend on metabolic changes within metastatic tumor cells, but few details are known. Here we report that expression of the enzyme γ-butyrobetaine hydroxylase 1 (BBOX1) under the control of a super enhancer allows a subset of cells in various types of tumors to produce the metabolite cartinine, which inhibits the small GTPase RhoA in natural killer cells, preventing immunological synapse formation and thereby protecting the metastatic cells from destruction. Knocking down γ-butyrobetaine hydroxylase 1 in tumor cells promoted their destruction by natural killer cells in vitro and improved the efficacy of natural killer cell adoptive transfer therapy in vivo. These findings illustrate how a subpopulation of tumor cells hijacks the metabolite carnitine to evade immune surveillance in the tumor microenvironment. Targeting γ-butyrobetaine hydroxylase 1 may be a powerful anti-metastatic strategy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-05T06:04:52.069Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4hwv29mhcb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4hwv29mhcb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82072692"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82272854"}],"categories":["Metastasis","Tumor Metabolism","Tumor-Associated Immunosuppression","Natural Killer Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z5x76wf2t6","title":"Immunoinformatics-Driven Design of a Multi-Epitope Subunit Vaccine Against Human Rhinovirus A for Pediatric Respiratory and Asthmatic Conditions","description":"These supplementary data document an immunoinformatics workflow for designing a multi-epitope subunit vaccine against Human Rhinovirus A, with relevance to pediatric respiratory disease and asthma. The datasets include sequence-similarity results for viral capsid proteins, predicted linear B-cell epitopes with residue-level scores, and extensive MHC class I and II binding predictions across multiple HLA alleles. Together, these analyses identify candidate antigenic regions capable of supporting both humoral and cellular immune recognition. The accompanying C-IMMSIM output evaluates the vaccine construct computationally by tracking B-cell, helper and cytotoxic T-cell, natural killer cell, macrophage, dendritic-cell, antibody, cytokine, and interleukin responses over time. Overall, the data provide systematic computational evidence for epitope selection, HLA coverage, construct immunogenicity, and simulated immune-response potential in a vaccine-development framework.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-06T21:45:36.205Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z5x76wf2t6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z5x76wf2t6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nada H. ","last_name":"Aljarba","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aniruddha ","last_name":"Chatterjee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arghya ","last_name":"Bhattacharya","orcid":null},{"first_name":" Jayadratha ","last_name":"Ghosh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Renuka S ","last_name":"Jyothi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel Ejim","last_name":"Uti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Esther Ugo","last_name":"Alum","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Respirovirus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cx2mxszth9","title":"γδ T_imaging data","description":"Epigenetic modulation of immune synaptic-cytoskeletal networks potentiates γδ T cell-mediated cytotoxicity in lung cancer","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:44:34.841Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cx2mxszth9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cx2mxszth9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hsing-Chen","last_name":"Tsai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cellular Immunity","Epigenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y59s4xhpzn","title":"Human innate immune cytokine response to dengue virus","description":"Fluorescence minus four controls for flow cytometry experiments of innate immune cytokine production in response  to dengue virus. Pseudo color flow cytometry plots for a representative patient comparing the fluorescence minus four (FMF; top row) control with completely stained sample (bottom) for secretion of (A) TNF-α, (B) IP-10, (C) IL-10, (D) IL-6 and (E) IFN-γ from the indicated cell subsets. Abbreviations: CM – CD14+CD16- classical monocytes; IM – CD14+CD16+ intermediate monocytes; NCM – CD14-CD16+ non-classical monocytes; G – Granulocytes; NKT – CD56+CD3+ natural killer T cells; NK++ - CD56+CD16+ natural killer cells; NK+- - CD56+CD16- natural killer cells; B – CD19+ cells. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-03T07:38:23.625Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y59s4xhpzn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y59s4xhpzn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Vijaya","last_name":"Satchidanandam","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Flow Cytometry","Innate Immunity","Dengue Fever"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y395v54y8k","title":"GPCR signaling contributes to immune characteristics of microenvironment and process of EBV-induced lymphomagenesis","description":"Natural killer T-cell lymphoma (NKTCL) is an aggressive and heterogeneous lymphoid neoplasm of natural killer or cytotoxic T cells in association with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. In the present study, we performed a comprehensive proteogenomic characterization of 112 NKTCL, incorporating genomics, transcriptomics, and deep-scale proteomics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-22T07:19:27.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y395v54y8k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y395v54y8k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mck239rj7k","title":"SLC1A1-mediated glutamine addiction contributes to natural killer T-cell lymphoma progression with therapeutic potential","description":"RNA sequencing data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-23T14:51:23.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mck239rj7k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mck239rj7k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jsjh28yhfc","title":"Processed RNA-seq gene expression and enrichment analysis data from [Role of NK Cells in Immune Regulation and Sepsis-Induced Inflammation]","description":"Research Hypothesis and Data Description:\n\nThe dataset was generated to investigate the role of NK (natural killer) cells in immune regulation during sepsis-induced inflammation. We hypothesized that NK cells modulate the expression of key immune-related genes and pathways, affecting inflammation and tissue response during sepsis.\n\nData Summary:\n\nThe dataset consists of processed RNA-seq gene expression data from NK cells isolated from control and sepsis models.\n\nIt includes normalized gene expression matrices, lists of differentially expressed genes (DEGs), and functional enrichment analysis results (GO and KEGG pathways).\n\nVisualizations such as heatmaps, barplots, and volcano plots are also included to summarize gene expression patterns and enriched pathways.\n\nNotable Findings:\n\nNK cells in sepsis models show significant changes in genes involved in cytokine signaling, immune response, and inflammatory pathways.\n\nEnrichment analysis highlights key pathways potentially regulated by NK cells, such as TNF signaling, NF-κB pathway, and interferon-mediated responses.\nThe data suggests that NK cells may have both pro- and anti-inflammatory roles in sepsis progression, providing insights into immune modulation mechanisms.\n\nData Interpretation and Use:\n\nResearchers can use this dataset to explore NK cell-specific transcriptional changes, identify novel targets for immunomodulation, or perform comparative analysis with other immune cell types.\nThe processed gene expression and enrichment results allow reanalysis, integration with other datasets, and functional interpretation without needing access to raw sequencing files.\nThis dataset is particularly useful for studying immune regulation, inflammation, and cell-specific responses in sepsis models.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-30T16:33:49.279Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jsjh28yhfc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jsjh28yhfc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aneela","last_name":"Bashir","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Muhammad Asif","last_name":"Mrza","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Immunology","Life Sciences","Genomics","Bioinformatics","Transcriptomics","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wwm9xv56ry","title":"Flow cytometry data for male breast cancer sample","description":"Male breast cancer (MBC) is a rare but aggressive malignancy with poor outcomes. The cellular states and immunological characteristics of MBC remain unclear. We performed a comparative analysis for transcriptomic data of 111,038 cells from six ER+ MBC and thirteen ER+ female breast cancer (FBC) samples. Single-cell T cell receptor sequencing (scTCR-seq) was also used to explore the difference of T cell clones between MBC and FBC. Results of single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq), bulk transcriptome, and immunohistochemistry consistently demonstrated that MBC had a significantly lower degree of T cell infiltration than FBC. Different characteristics of T cell subpopulations between MBC and FBC were identified. T cells in MBC showed activation of p38 MAPK and lipid oxidation pathways, indicating the dysfunctional state. In contrast, T cells in FBC exhibited a higher expression level of cytotoxic markers such as GZMK and KLRB1, and activated pathways mediated by immune-modulatory cytokines. Moreover, we identified the inhibitory interactions between cancer cells and T cells in the MBC microenvironment, such as cell-cell communications mediated by TGF-β, TIGIT, and VSIR. Our study will provide important information for understanding the tumor immunology and metabolism of MBC, and promote the development of effective treatments for MBC. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:31:43.211Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wwm9xv56ry","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wwm9xv56ry"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kening","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Flow Cytometry","T Cell","Male Breast Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n5jf5vppk4","title":"Normal Cell Differentiation Potential of Cancer Stem Cells Without Reprogramming Pluripotent Factors: a Novel Strategy in Stem Cell-Based Therapy for Tissue Regeneration ","description":"     Stem cells hold great promise for tissue regeneration and have the potential to treat many incurable degenerative diseases. Cancer stem cells (CSCs), or cancer initiating cells, have the ability to self-renew and differentiate into heterogeneous lineages of cancer cells. Current stem cell therapies face limitations, such as limited stem cell sources, time consumption, tumor formation, and immune rejection upon allogeneic transplantation. Allogeneic stem cell treatments simplify stem cell manufacturing and reduce transplant time, but their therapeutic potential is limited by human leukocyte antigen (HLA)-matched donors. CSCs retain characteristics essential for tissue regeneration. However, several limitations hinder cancer stem cell reprogramming with pluripotent factors. The development of 3D culture models for tissue imitating extracellular matrix in cancer cell lines aims to enhance CSC enrichment. This mini-review focuses on a new strategy for treating incurable degenerative diseases involving in vitro and in vivo 3D cancer models and the induced differentiation of CSCs into mature normal cell types. This allows tissue survival without immune rejection and offers a safe alternative to cancer stem cell reprogramming with pluripotent factors. In conclusion, preservation and banking of allogeneic CSCs offer an alternative, readily available, and safe strategy that can be used to facilitate stem cell-based cell therapy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-01T06:32:29.797Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n5jf5vppk4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n5jf5vppk4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Josita Alloysius ","last_name":"Josita","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Banking","Stem Cells Research","Regenerative Medicine","Induced Pluripotent Stem Cell","Cancer Stem Cell","Degenerative Deformity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yb7wftjchk","title":"Merkel Cell Carcinoma Supplementary Data","description":"Supplementary Materials for Comparative response patterns to immune checkpoint inhibition in Merkel cell carcinoma: A Merkel cell polyomavirus-stratified systematic review\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-18T07:50:32.167Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yb7wftjchk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yb7wftjchk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andy","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medicine"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":129989,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":142,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":129989,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":129464,"restricted":63,"embargoed":462},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2169},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1335},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":611},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":458},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":433},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":411},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":360},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":298},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":267}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":127383},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1897},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":182},{"id":"b070